1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,460
Flip a switch, and the lamp is on before your finger has

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:05,700
left the button.

3
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:08,700
So the electrons must race through the wire.

4
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:09,270
Right?

5
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:10,910
Let's go inside and look.

6
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:11,990
Here they are.

7
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:13,750
And they are barely moving.

8
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:17,100
In an ordinary copper wire carrying one ampere,

9
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:20,060
the electrons drift forward at less than a tenth of a

10
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,150
millimetre every second.

11
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:25,270
A snail is more than ten times faster.

12
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:27,860
To travel a single metre of this wire,

13
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:30,950
an electron needs nearly four hours.

14
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,620
So how does the lamp light instantly?

15
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:36,780
And what is holding the electrons back so badly?

16
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:39,420
The answer to both is one idea:

17
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:40,807
resistance.

18
00:00:40,827 --> 00:00:43,660
By the end of this video you will be able to see it,

19
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:45,190
not just calculate it.

20
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:51,850
This is copper, magnified a hundred million times.

21
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:54,160
The copper atoms sit in a neat,

22
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:56,690
repeating grid called a lattice.

23
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:00,050
Each atom has let go of one outer electron.

24
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:04,010
Those freed electrons belong to no atom in particular.

25
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:07,160
They wander through the whole metal like a gas,

26
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:09,730
and there is an enormous number of them:

27
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:13,330
about eighty-five billion billion in a speck of copper

28
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:14,970
one millimetre wide.

29
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:16,770
And they are not resting.

30
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:18,920
Even with no battery connected,

31
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:22,120
every electron is flying around at more than a thousand

32
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:23,770
kilometres per second.

33
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:25,650
But the directions are random.

34
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:28,050
For every electron heading left,

35
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:29,690
another is heading right.

36
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:32,770
Add them all up and the net flow is zero.

37
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:34,010
No current.

38
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:35,970
Now connect a battery.

39
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:38,880
It sets up an electric field along the wire,

40
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:43,000
and every electron feels a gentle push in one direction.

41
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:46,240
The frantic random motion carries on as before,

42
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:50,040
but now the whole crowd leans, ever so slightly,

43
00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:51,080
one way.

44
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:54,040
That slow lean is called drift.

45
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:56,280
And drift is the current.

46
00:01:56,300 --> 00:01:58,520
This also solves our paradox.

47
00:01:58,540 --> 00:02:01,080
The wire is already full of electrons,

48
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:02,600
from end to end.

49
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:05,800
Push at one end, and the push is passed along at close

50
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:07,400
to the speed of light.

51
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:10,200
Think of a pipe that is already full of water.

52
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:14,000
Open the tap, and water comes out of the far end at once,

53
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:17,650
even though the water that just entered is still far behind.

54
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:19,250
The electrons crawl.

55
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:21,250
The message flies.

56
00:02:22,633 --> 00:02:26,093
If the field keeps pushing, why don't the electrons keep

57
00:02:26,113 --> 00:02:27,303
getting faster?

58
00:02:27,433 --> 00:02:29,863
Because they keep being interrupted.

59
00:02:29,993 --> 00:02:32,893
The lattice is never perfectly still.

60
00:02:32,913 --> 00:02:36,613
Its atoms tremble with heat, and here and there sits an

61
00:02:36,633 --> 00:02:38,533
impurity or a flaw.

62
00:02:38,553 --> 00:02:41,493
An electron picks up a little speed from the field,

63
00:02:41,513 --> 00:02:44,613
then scatters off one of these disturbances and flies

64
00:02:44,633 --> 00:02:46,533
off in a random direction.

65
00:02:46,553 --> 00:02:48,813
Its forward progress is wiped out,

66
00:02:48,833 --> 00:02:50,853
and it has to start again.

67
00:02:50,873 --> 00:02:53,333
Gain, scatter, reset.

68
00:02:53,353 --> 00:02:55,773
Gain, scatter, reset.

69
00:02:55,793 --> 00:02:59,143
Tens of trillions of times every second.

70
00:02:59,273 --> 00:03:02,053
Here is something most textbooks leave out.

71
00:03:02,073 --> 00:03:05,503
The electrons are not simply bumping into the atoms.

72
00:03:05,593 --> 00:03:09,013
A perfectly regular, perfectly still lattice would let

73
00:03:09,033 --> 00:03:10,703
them glide straight through.

74
00:03:10,873 --> 00:03:13,453
What scatters them is the disorder:

75
00:03:13,473 --> 00:03:15,423
the trembling, and the flaws.

76
00:03:15,833 --> 00:03:16,853
Keep that in mind.

77
00:03:16,873 --> 00:03:18,183
It matters later.

78
00:03:18,313 --> 00:03:21,463
This constant interruption is resistance.

79
00:03:21,513 --> 00:03:25,263
It is how hard a conductor makes it to keep charge flowing.

80
00:03:25,593 --> 00:03:28,613
We measure it by asking a simple question:

81
00:03:28,633 --> 00:03:32,023
how much push do I need for each unit of flow?

82
00:03:32,153 --> 00:03:34,973
The push is voltage, in volts.

83
00:03:34,993 --> 00:03:37,813
The flow is current, in amperes.

84
00:03:37,833 --> 00:03:40,693
Divide one by the other and you get resistance,

85
00:03:40,713 --> 00:03:42,013
in ohms.

86
00:03:42,033 --> 00:03:45,973
If one volt drives one ampere, that is one ohm.

87
00:03:45,993 --> 00:03:48,613
If you need ten volts for that same ampere,

88
00:03:48,633 --> 00:03:50,383
that is ten ohms.

89
00:03:50,433 --> 00:03:54,623
This is Ohm's law: V equals I times R.

90
00:03:55,967 --> 00:03:59,157
Now let's change the wire and watch what happens.

91
00:03:59,487 --> 00:04:01,637
First, make it twice as long.

92
00:04:01,967 --> 00:04:05,427
Every electron must now get through twice as much lattice,

93
00:04:05,447 --> 00:04:08,357
and is interrupted twice as many times along the way.

94
00:04:08,607 --> 00:04:11,477
For the same current, you need twice the push.

95
00:04:11,807 --> 00:04:14,197
Double the length, double the resistance.

96
00:04:14,607 --> 00:04:18,227
Next, go back, and make the wire thicker instead,

97
00:04:18,247 --> 00:04:20,037
with twice the cross-section.

98
00:04:20,127 --> 00:04:23,387
Now there are twice as many electrons side by side,

99
00:04:23,407 --> 00:04:26,427
all drifting together, like a road that has gone from

100
00:04:26,447 --> 00:04:27,987
two lanes to four.

101
00:04:28,007 --> 00:04:30,787
The same push moves twice the charge.

102
00:04:30,807 --> 00:04:33,467
Double the area, half the resistance.

103
00:04:33,487 --> 00:04:35,787
Notice that it is the area that counts,

104
00:04:35,807 --> 00:04:37,147
not the diameter.

105
00:04:37,167 --> 00:04:40,667
Double the diameter and the area becomes four times bigger,

106
00:04:40,687 --> 00:04:43,147
so the resistance falls to a quarter.

107
00:04:43,167 --> 00:04:45,227
So resistance grows with length,

108
00:04:45,247 --> 00:04:47,877
L, and shrinks with area, A.

109
00:04:48,207 --> 00:04:50,347
Engineers use this every day.

110
00:04:50,367 --> 00:04:53,707
A thin extension cord warms up under a heavy load,

111
00:04:53,727 --> 00:04:55,747
while a thick one stays cool.

112
00:04:55,767 --> 00:04:58,707
And a strain gauge, the sensor that weighs trucks and

113
00:04:58,727 --> 00:05:02,117
watches over bridges, is just a very fine wire.

114
00:05:02,207 --> 00:05:05,067
Stretch it, and it becomes longer and thinner,

115
00:05:05,087 --> 00:05:08,357
so its resistance rises by an amount you can measure.

116
00:05:09,733 --> 00:05:13,083
But length and area are not the whole story.

117
00:05:13,253 --> 00:05:16,353
Take two wires of exactly the same size,

118
00:05:16,373 --> 00:05:19,393
one made of copper and one of nichrome,

119
00:05:19,413 --> 00:05:21,563
the alloy inside a heater coil.

120
00:05:21,733 --> 00:05:25,523
The nichrome has about sixty-five times the resistance.

121
00:05:25,653 --> 00:05:26,643
Same shape.

122
00:05:26,693 --> 00:05:27,803
Different material.

123
00:05:28,213 --> 00:05:31,553
So we need a number that describes the material alone,

124
00:05:31,573 --> 00:05:33,083
with the shape taken out.

125
00:05:33,413 --> 00:05:36,923
That number is resistivity, written with the Greek letter

126
00:05:37,013 --> 00:05:37,563
rho.

127
00:05:37,893 --> 00:05:40,593
And now the formula is complete:

128
00:05:40,613 --> 00:05:43,723
R equals rho, times L over A.

129
00:05:44,133 --> 00:05:45,163
Read it like this.

130
00:05:45,573 --> 00:05:48,043
Rho is what the material contributes.

131
00:05:48,133 --> 00:05:50,843
L over A is what the shape contributes.

132
00:05:51,413 --> 00:05:53,163
What is rho, physically?

133
00:05:53,653 --> 00:05:55,633
Picture a cube of the material,

134
00:05:55,653 --> 00:05:57,243
one metre on each side.

135
00:05:57,573 --> 00:06:00,873
The resistance from one face to the opposite face is its

136
00:06:00,893 --> 00:06:01,803
resistivity.

137
00:06:02,133 --> 00:06:05,083
That is why its unit is the ohm metre.

138
00:06:05,253 --> 00:06:07,083
Here is the distinction to remember.

139
00:06:07,413 --> 00:06:09,873
Resistance belongs to an object:

140
00:06:09,893 --> 00:06:11,643
this wire, this coil.

141
00:06:11,973 --> 00:06:14,753
Resistivity belongs to a substance:

142
00:06:14,773 --> 00:06:17,243
copper, anywhere, in any shape.

143
00:06:17,573 --> 00:06:19,403
You will also meet its inverse,

144
00:06:19,493 --> 00:06:21,403
conductivity, written sigma.

145
00:06:21,893 --> 00:06:23,243
Now look at the range.

146
00:06:23,573 --> 00:06:27,313
Silver is the best conductor, with copper just behind,

147
00:06:27,333 --> 00:06:29,403
then gold and aluminium.

148
00:06:29,573 --> 00:06:32,443
Nichrome is about sixty-five times copper.

149
00:06:32,853 --> 00:06:36,603
Pure silicon is more than a hundred billion times copper.

150
00:06:36,933 --> 00:06:40,033
And glass: a billion billion times,

151
00:06:40,053 --> 00:06:41,003
and beyond.

152
00:06:41,493 --> 00:06:45,163
Few properties in all of physics span a range this wide.

153
00:06:45,413 --> 00:06:47,553
And it comes down to two things:

154
00:06:47,573 --> 00:06:49,873
how many electrons are free to move,

155
00:06:49,893 --> 00:06:52,043
and how easily they get through.

156
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:56,020
Resistivity is not fixed, though.

157
00:06:56,040 --> 00:06:59,790
It changes with temperature, and you already know why.

158
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:03,870
Heat a metal and its lattice trembles more violently.

159
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:06,340
More trembling means more scattering,

160
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:09,070
so the electrons are interrupted more often.

161
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:10,990
Resistance goes up.

162
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:14,660
For copper, it rises about four tenths of a percent for

163
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:16,430
every degree Celsius.

164
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:19,910
A tungsten lamp filament shows this dramatically.

165
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:23,020
Glowing at well over two thousand degrees,

166
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:26,100
its resistance is more than ten times what it was when

167
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:26,670
cold.

168
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:30,460
That is why a filament bulb draws a surge of current at

169
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:32,350
the instant you switch it on.

170
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:35,510
Engineers turn this effect into a thermometer.

171
00:07:35,640 --> 00:07:40,420
A platinum sensor called a PT100 has exactly one hundred

172
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:41,950
ohms at zero degrees.

173
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:44,990
Measure its resistance and you know its temperature.

174
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:48,340
Generators and large motors have these buried in their

175
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:49,150
windings.

176
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:52,590
Semiconductors such as silicon do the opposite.

177
00:07:52,920 --> 00:07:55,420
Heat shakes more electrons free,

178
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:58,340
and that gain outweighs the extra scattering,

179
00:07:58,360 --> 00:08:00,950
so their resistance falls as they warm.

180
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:03,500
And cool certain materials far enough,

181
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:06,590
and the resistance drops to exactly zero.

182
00:08:06,760 --> 00:08:10,660
That is superconductivity, and it is a story for another

183
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:11,310
day.

184
00:08:12,667 --> 00:08:14,417
One question is left.

185
00:08:14,587 --> 00:08:16,847
Every time an electron scatters,

186
00:08:16,867 --> 00:08:20,407
it loses the energy the field has just given it.

187
00:08:20,427 --> 00:08:21,857
Where does that energy go?

188
00:08:22,267 --> 00:08:23,857
Into the lattice.

189
00:08:24,027 --> 00:08:27,767
Each scattering makes the atoms tremble a little harder,

190
00:08:27,787 --> 00:08:30,647
and trembling atoms are, quite literally,

191
00:08:30,667 --> 00:08:31,537
heat.

192
00:08:31,707 --> 00:08:35,337
So every conductor that carries a current warms up.

193
00:08:35,467 --> 00:08:38,647
The power turned into heat is the current squared,

194
00:08:38,667 --> 00:08:39,857
times the resistance.

195
00:08:40,187 --> 00:08:42,417
P equals I squared R.

196
00:08:42,827 --> 00:08:45,697
Sometimes that heat is the whole point.

197
00:08:46,027 --> 00:08:49,767
A heater, a toaster and a lamp filament are made from

198
00:08:49,787 --> 00:08:53,697
high-resistivity material so that they get hot on purpose.

199
00:08:54,027 --> 00:08:57,607
A fuse is a thin wire designed to melt when the current

200
00:08:57,627 --> 00:09:00,967
is too high, breaking the circuit before anything else

201
00:09:00,987 --> 00:09:02,097
is damaged.

202
00:09:02,267 --> 00:09:05,817
But in a power line, that heat is pure loss.

203
00:09:05,947 --> 00:09:07,297
And notice the square.

204
00:09:07,627 --> 00:09:10,857
Halve the current and the loss falls to a quarter.

205
00:09:10,987 --> 00:09:14,247
That is why electricity crosses the country at hundreds

206
00:09:14,267 --> 00:09:16,017
of thousands of volts.

207
00:09:16,107 --> 00:09:19,687
For the same power, ten times the voltage needs only one

208
00:09:19,707 --> 00:09:23,287
tenth of the current, and loses just one hundredth as

209
00:09:23,307 --> 00:09:24,257
much as heat.

210
00:09:24,587 --> 00:09:28,017
It is also why every cable has a current rating.

211
00:09:28,107 --> 00:09:30,487
Push more through it than it was sized for,

212
00:09:30,507 --> 00:09:32,737
and the heat has nowhere to go.

213
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:36,130
So let's put it all together.

214
00:09:36,260 --> 00:09:38,970
A metal is full of free electrons,

215
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:40,490
always in motion.

216
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:42,690
A voltage makes them drift.

217
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:46,200
The disorder of the lattice keeps interrupting that drift,

218
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:48,010
and that is resistance.

219
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:50,210
A longer path raises it.

220
00:09:50,260 --> 00:09:52,170
A wider path lowers it.

221
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:55,730
The material sets the scale, through its resistivity.

222
00:09:55,780 --> 00:09:58,480
In metals, heat raises it further.

223
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:02,330
And every interruption turns electrical energy into heat.

224
00:10:02,660 --> 00:10:06,240
All of that, in one line: R equals rho,

225
00:10:06,260 --> 00:10:07,370
L over A.

226
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:09,480
Next time you meet that formula,

227
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:10,570
don't just read it.

228
00:10:10,980 --> 00:10:12,250
See the wire.
